Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether weak-star convergence $f_n \xrightarrow{wk\star} f$ in $L^p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ implies $\int f_n g \to \int f g$ for all $g \in L^q(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with $1/p + 1/q = 1$.

**Answer: Yes, this follows directly from the definition of weak-star convergence.**

## Proof

**Setup.** Weak-star convergence is defined on a dual space $X^*$. For the Lebesgue spaces on $\mathbb{R}^d$, the key duality fact is:

$$L^p(\mathbb{R}^d) = \big(L^q(\mathbb{R}^d)\big)^* \quad \text{when } 1 < p \leq \infty,\; 1/p + 1/q = 1,\; 1 \leq q < \infty,$$

where the duality pairing is given by

$$\langle f, g \rangle = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f(x)\, g(x)\, dx, \quad f \in L^p,\; g \in L^q.$$

This is the standard Riesz representation theorem for $L^q$ with $1 \leq q < \infty$: every continuous linear functional on $L^q$ is of the form $g \mapsto \int f g$ for a unique $f \in L^p$.

**Definition of weak-star convergence.** Let $X$ be a Banach space and $X^*$ its dual. A sequence $\{f_n\} \subset X^*$ converges to $f \in X^*$ in the weak-star topology, written $f_n \xrightarrow{wk\star} f$, if and only if

$$\langle f_n, x \rangle \to \langle f, x \rangle \quad \text{for every } x \in X,$$

where $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$ denotes the duality pairing between $X^*$ and $X$.

**Application to $L^p$.** Taking $X = L^q(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $X^* = L^p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ (valid for $1 < p \leq \infty$, $1 \leq q < \infty$, $1/p + 1/q = 1$), the definition specializes to:

$$f_n \xrightarrow{wk\star} f \text{ in } L^p(\mathbb{R}^d) \iff \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f_n(x)\, g(x)\, dx \to \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f(x)\, g(x)\, dx \quad \text{for every } g \in L^q(\mathbb{R}^d).$$

**Conclusion.** The convergence $\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f_n g \to \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} f g$ for all $g \in L^q(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is not merely a consequence of weak-star convergence — it is the very **definition** of weak-star convergence in $L^p(\mathbb{R}^d) = (L^q(\mathbb{R}^d))^*$. The weak-star topology is precisely the coarsest topology on $L^p$ that makes all the maps $f \mapsto \int f g$ (for $g \in L^q$) continuous, so convergence in this topology is exactly pointwise convergence of all these evaluation functionals.

**Remark on the range of $p$.** This duality $L^p = (L^q)^*$ holds for $1 < p \leq \infty$ (equivalently $1 \leq q < \infty$). For $p = 1$ (i.e., $q = \infty$), $L^1$ is not the dual of $L^\infty$ in general, so weak-star convergence in $L^1$ is not defined via this duality. The two notable cases are:
- **$p = \infty$, $q = 1$:** $L^\infty = (L^1)^*$, and weak-star convergence $f_n \xrightarrow{wk\star} f$ in $L^\infty$ means $\int f_n g \to \int f g$ for all $g \in L^1$. This is the most common setting where the $wk\star$ terminology is used, since $L^\infty$ is not reflexive and the weak-star topology is strictly weaker than the weak topology.
- **$1 < p < \infty$:** $L^p$ is reflexive, so weak-star convergence coincides with weak convergence, and again $\int f_n g \to \int f g$ for all $g \in L^q$ by definition.

In all cases where weak-star convergence in $L^p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is meaningful, the desired conclusion holds **by definition**. $\blacksquare$

